<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qaitbay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1416–<a href="/years/1496">1496</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Al-Ashraf Qaitbay</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qaitbay was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from 1468 to 1496, one of the longest and most stable reigns of the Circassian period. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> to a standstill in a war of 1485–1491 and was a great builder, leaving his funerary complex in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>'s Northern Cemetery and the Citadel of Qaitbay at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. Heavy military spending and plague strained his treasury.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qaitbay.jpg" alt="Qaitbay" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Florentine painter Cristofano dell'Altissimo, 16th century · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mamluk_Sultan_Kayitbay_by_Florentine_painter_Cristofano_dell%27Altissimo_Galleria_degli_Uffizi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Qaitbay was born between 1416 and 1418 in Circassia in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. Enslaved, he was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> around 1440 and purchased by the sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barsbay">Barsbay</a>, who assigned him to the palace guard. Barsbay's successor Jaqmaq freed him after learning that he was a descendant of the Ayyubid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> al-Ashraf Musa. He rose through the military hierarchy to command 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>, and under Timurbugha he became atabak, or field marshal, of the whole army. Timurbugha was deposed in a palace coup on 30 January 1468, and Qaitbay, proposed as a compromise candidate acceptable to the court factions, was made sultan on 31 January.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Qaitbay exiled the leaders of the coup and appointed rivals to posts of equal authority, so that no subordinate could gather too much power. His first major challenge was the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-suwar">Shah Suwar</a>, a Dhu'l-Qadrid leader in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> armies were defeated before a third routed him in 1471, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suvar">Suwar</a> was captured in 1473 and put to death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. Qaitbay made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> in 1472, and about 1480 he fortified the ruins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lighthouse-of-alexandria">Lighthouse of Alexandria</a>; the fort became the Citadel of Qaitbay.</p><p>From 1485 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> campaigned on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> frontier. A Mamluk army won a victory near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adana">Adana</a> in 1486 and another when the Ottomans reoccupied the town in 1487, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a> allied with the Mamluks against the Ottomans from 1488 until 1491. A final truce signed in 1491 lasted through the reigns of Qaitbay and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Bayezid II</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The last years brought unrest among the troops and the sultan's declining health. Plague returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1492 and was reported to have claimed 200,000 lives. Qaitbay died in August 1496 and was buried in the mausoleum he had built in Cairo's Northern Cemetery. A succession crisis of five years followed before the accession of al-Ashraf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qansuh-al-ghawri">Qansuh al-Ghawri</a>.</p><p>His reign has traditionally been seen as the culmination of the Burji Mamluk dynasty, though he may be criticised for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a> and for failing to innovate. At least 230 monuments are associated with his reign, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, Gaza, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, where he renewed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque">Mosque of the Prophet</a> after a fire in 1481. His funerary complex is considered a masterpiece of late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> architecture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qaitbay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Qalawun, al-Nasir Muhammad, Sultan Hasan and later <b>Qaitbay</b> filled Cairo with works of Islamic architecture, while scholars such as Ibn Khaldun, al-Maqrizi and Ibn Hajar made it a centre of learning.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It broke, and was later built into the foundations of a fortress raised by the Mamluk sultan <b>Qaitbay</b> at Rashid.</blockquote><a href="/work/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
